No—“can work for some people” does not imply “will work for essentially everyone.”

That difference is not cherry-picking; it is about individual anatomy and surgical risk. Two people can both be “large” but differ greatly in:

- how much fat is under the skin versus around internal organs;
- how much total fat would need to be lost to reach a thin size;
- skin elasticity and amount of loose skin likely after removal;
- diabetes, sleep apnea, heart/lung disease, clotting risk, prior surgery, etc.;
- whether the fat is in areas that can be treated safely and evenly.

Multi-area liposuction can make certain appropriate candidates dramatically slimmer. It cannot safely be assumed to make *any given* large person thin.
